1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,663
External truckers should hit one hostname
— https://ops.harbor.example — not a
spreadsheet of gate, billing, and
scheduling URLs. TLS terminates once.
Authn checks once.

2
00:00:14,783 --> 00:00:22,128
Paths fan out to internals that never
appear on the public DNS. That front door
is an API gateway.

3
00:00:22,247 --> 00:00:32,975
Spring Cloud Gateway is a reactive Boot
application built on WebFlux and a route
model: predicates decide whether a request
matches; filters reshape headers, paths,
or responses;

4
00:00:33,095 --> 00:00:39,479
the URI — often lb://gate-service — sends
traffic to a discovered instance.

5
00:00:39,599 --> 00:00:47,255
It is not a servlet RestController that
proxies with RestTemplate. Think route
tables, not controller methods.

6
00:00:47,375 --> 00:00:51,119
Under load the gateway’s event loop stays
non-blocking;

7
00:00:51,239 --> 00:01:00,719
a blocking call inside a filter starves
other truckers’ requests on the same
threads — that is why Gateway sits on
WebFlux even when gate and billing remain
servlet Boot apps.

8
00:01:00,839 --> 00:01:14,183
Look at this example. spring: Look at this
java for a moment. A trucker POSTs
/api/gates/G12/check-ins. The Path
predicate matches gate-checkins.

9
00:01:14,303 --> 00:01:27,767
StripPrefix=1 drops /api (or your
configured count) so gate sees the path it
actually mapped. lb://gate-service
resolves through discovery and load
balancing to a concrete pod.

10
00:01:27,887 --> 00:01:39,503
If StripPrefix is wrong by one segment,
gate returns 404 while the gateway log
still shows a successful route match —
classic symptom: edge 200 path rewritten
into an unknown controller mapping.

11
00:01:39,623 --> 00:01:48,768
Billing and scheduling stay unreachable
except through their declared paths; a
curious client probing
/api/internal/ledger should not find a
route.

12
00:01:48,887 --> 00:01:57,671
Global filters can attach correlation
headers, enforce JWT at the edge, or rate-
limit abusive clients before a gate pod
burns CPU.

13
00:01:57,791 --> 00:02:05,495
Walk a RateLimiter trip: Redis says the
trucker token bucket is empty; Gateway
returns 429 without calling gate.

14
00:02:05,615 --> 00:02:15,719
Walk a downstream outage: circuit breaker
filter opens, fallback URI returns a
controlled JSON body, booth scanners show
“retry shortly” instead of a raw
connection reset.

15
00:02:15,839 --> 00:02:29,087
Those behaviors belong at the edge when
every public client shares one hostname.
Keep domain logic out of the gateway.
Quoting tariffs, releasing cargo,
assigning berths — those belong in
services.

16
00:02:29,207 --> 00:02:37,823
The gateway owns technical cross-cuts:
routing, TLS, coarse auth, request size,
maybe canary headers (X-Harbor-Canary →
weighted routes).

17
00:02:37,943 --> 00:02:49,511
When the gateway accumulates business
rules — “hazard class IMDG_3 must go to
billing first” — you have rebuilt the
monolith at the wrong layer, and every
change to tariff policy requires an edge
deploy.

18
00:02:49,631 --> 00:03:03,935
Trade-offs: one gateway simplifies trucker
TLS and WAF placement, but it is also a
blast radius — a bad route YAML takes down
all three backends’ public face. Some
teams split edge by audience (trucker vs
ops console).

19
00:03:04,055 --> 00:03:17,207
Latency adds a hop; measure it. Predicate
order matters when two routes could match;
put the more specific path first or you
will debug “why does GET /api/berths/B7
hit the wrong service?” for an hour.

20
00:03:17,327 --> 00:03:24,839
A misconception is exposing every internal
Actuator through the gateway “for
convenience” — that convenience is an
attack surface.

21
00:03:24,959 --> 00:03:32,063
Another is confusing Gateway with Spring
MVC reverse-proxy hacks: predicates and
filters are the programming model.

22
00:03:32,183 --> 00:03:42,887
A third is putting sticky session affinity
on by default for a stateless check-in
API; prefer token-based identity and any
healthy instance. Truckers now have one
edge.

23
00:03:43,007 --> 00:03:54,575
Behind it, three billing pods answer the
same service id. Who chooses which pod
gets the next quote request? Client-side
load balancing makes that choice explicit.
